00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.550
MELANIE WARRICK: So Yabebal,
why don't we start with you?

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:07.650
Can you tell us about who
you are and your background?

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:10.120
YABEBAL FANTAYE: So yeah,
I'm Yabebal Fantaye,

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.250
and I grew up in
Ethiopia, Addis Ababa,

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.810
and I did my undergraduate
studies in Addis Ababa.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:20.570
And then I went to Cape Town
for my Master's, and then I

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.700
went to Italy for my PhD.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:25.100
And then did some
post-docs in Europe

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.640
and came back now to Africa
with something called the Robert

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:29.480
Bosch Foundation.

00:05:29.480 --> 00:05:32.720
They funded this research
here called ARETÉ AIMS Chair,

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.020
which is really unusual funding
because it allows you to work

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.170
anywhere in Africa.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.310
And so I am really
enjoying being

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:44.040
now Pan-African, practically,
not just talking about it.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.510
So I am doing research in
areas of cosmology, which

00:05:47.510 --> 00:05:51.020
is my main speciality, and
also using satellite images

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:55.110
to try to understand-- is Africa
going in the right direction,

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:58.460
and can we get that not only
in the official statistics,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.880
but also through some mining the
data, which is available free,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:02.810
satellite images.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:05.070
So those are kind of
background, I would say.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:06.320
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.120
And I know when we were talking
before you were telling me

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:09.290
about some of the
travels that you

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.150
do for work, which is
wonderful that you get

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:12.710
to call yourself Pan-African.

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:14.918
YABEBAL FANTAYE: I think
now I can call it like that,

00:06:14.918 --> 00:06:16.282
and it's really different.

00:06:16.282 --> 00:06:18.740
When you are really doing it,
it's actually much different,

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:22.110
because you really start
seeing the similarities.

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.520
And I think I'm now not
just philosophically only,

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.790
but practically, it is Africa
just as one makes sense.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.570
That was probably the reason
why people in the 1960s,

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:34.820
you know, when they were
talking about Pan-Africanism,

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:37.520
they actually probably
felt similar in a way.

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.660
We share a lot more similarities
than our differences.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:43.730
And our differences
just within Ethiopia,

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:47.960
we have so much difference
within different tribes

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.770
than probably I have with
other African countries.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.390
So in a way, you know, it's
just natural, that difference.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:54.710
It's our beauty.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:56.757
And we should just celebrate it.

00:06:56.757 --> 00:06:58.340
But at the same time,
our similarities

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.320
are a lot, which we
can capitalize on that.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.570
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:07:02.570 --> 00:07:05.455
And then, Jessica, can you
tell us a little bit about you?

00:07:05.455 --> 00:07:06.371
JESSICA PHALAFALA: OK.

00:07:06.371 --> 00:07:07.370
Thank you, Melanie.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:11.100
I was born in Polokwane
here in South Africa.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:15.380
And in terms of my studies,
I did my undergraduate

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:17.210
at Wits University.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:20.520
And that was in economics
and mathematics.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.500
I went on to do my post-grad to
Master's level at Wits as well.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:27.075
And my specialization was
in functional analysis.

00:07:27.075 --> 00:07:28.625
So I'm actually a
pure mathematician.

00:07:28.625 --> 00:07:29.390
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, wow.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:30.390
JESSICA PHALAFALA: Yeah.

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:34.460
And so during my
master's, I realized

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.470
that I would like to
use the skills that I

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:41.240
attained to work towards a
PhD that's a little bit more

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:42.650
applied.

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.710
And that's when I
explored data science.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:48.530
And then I moved to
the African Institute

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:50.300
for Mathematical
Sciences, and now I'm

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.180
doing my PhD in
applied mathematics.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:56.450
And the area of my research
is Manifold Learning

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.370
and Approximation Theory.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:00.680
So it does allow me to
use those functional

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.590
analysis and harmonic
analysis skills together

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:07.130
with some new skills, like
information theory and coding.

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:09.340
So it's been really a
wonderful experience.

00:08:09.340 --> 00:08:10.590
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.020
And so the Africa Institute
for Mathematical Science,

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:15.400
I know both of you are there.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:17.900
Can you tell me a little bit
more, tell us a little bit more

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.930
about what the institution
focuses on in terms

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:22.139
of the research that it does?

00:08:22.139 --> 00:08:23.054
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Yeah.

00:08:23.054 --> 00:08:25.100
So the African Institute
of Mathematical Science,

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:29.840
usually just AIMS, was
founded in 2003 by Neil Turok.

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:32.360
And I think the main
idea was, of course,

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.900
Neil Turok got those ideas
when he was traveling,

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:38.210
that he was inspired by how
people think differently

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:38.878
in Africa.

00:08:38.878 --> 00:08:40.669
And he believed that
their way of thinking,

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:43.130
the mathematical sciences,
should be the core.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:45.230
And that's where I
think his vision was

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.540
like, OK, let's have
everywhere in Africa,

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:49.672
not just in South
Africa or localized,

00:08:49.672 --> 00:08:51.380
but just everywhere
in Africa, let's have

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:54.900
like these examples that could
do in any of the mathematics,

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:56.840
physics or mathematics
or economics,

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.870
any of them which involves
some mathematical thinking,

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:05.270
to be leaders, and to try
and to be the first kind of--

00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:08.160
an example for others to follow.

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.520
So I think it
worked phenomenally.

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.380
And that's why we're still here.

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:16.130
And since even-- like it
was in South Africa only,

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:20.390
but then it expanded now to
six other countries in Africa.

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:24.710
And it's graduating, I
think, more than 1,500 people

00:09:24.710 --> 00:09:26.420
and from all over Africa.

00:09:26.420 --> 00:09:28.930
According to the map that
you see everywhere in Africa

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:30.980
is green, which means
we have graduates

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:32.900
from everywhere in Africa.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.160
And that is really great.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.473
And then the research
aspect came around 2007.

00:09:37.473 --> 00:09:40.820
It was started in South
Africa, the AIMS South Africa.

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:44.330
So that one involved lots
of researchers coming, some

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:47.240
of them just they are
temporally, some of them

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:50.930
are research scientists
there, but maybe affiliated

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:54.650
to other universities, for
example Stellenbosch, and also

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:55.820
University of Cape Town.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:58.550
So the kind of work
we do, I would say,

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:01.730
like are financial
mathematics researchers,

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.240
there are biomathematics,
and there is cosmology,

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:08.210
which I am part of, but
which also now evolves

00:10:08.210 --> 00:10:09.920
into the machine learning group.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.580
And also now the data science
group that Jessica is in.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:16.280
There are a lot more
coming and going also,

00:10:16.280 --> 00:10:17.826
which I probably may not cover.

00:10:17.826 --> 00:10:19.950
But every of the mathematical
science, I would say,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:20.940
is covered.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.930
And what makes AIMS special
is that actually there

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:26.770
are no lectures, per se.

00:10:26.770 --> 00:10:29.760
So it's not like a very
different, intensive, one-year,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.630
but a lot more introducing
people from everywhere

00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:36.960
in the world who are really
doing amazing research.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:39.560
They come and teach there
willingly, voluntarily.

00:10:39.560 --> 00:10:42.510
And they basically
leave with the students

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:44.780
in the three-weeks module.

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:47.370
And they instruct, and
basically just the students

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.400
learn not only the subject,
but the way of thinking, right?

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.180
So which makes AIMS, I
would say, very special.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:58.290
And then AIMS evolved into the
whole Next Einstein Initiative,

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:01.050
which is now centered
in Rwanda, and which

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:03.690
is managing not
only AIMS but also

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.940
there is the Next
Einstein Forum,

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:09.560
which every two years brings
together scientists, business

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.940
people, politicians in one.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:13.890
And this is the
first of its kind.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:15.900
There was no such big
gathering in Africa.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:19.020
And that's really where you see
I am a Next Einstein Fellow.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:22.740
And it highlights young people
doing something in research.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:24.870
And if they are excellent
in their research,

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.000
they bring them together,
and then they highlight.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.020
They give them that opportunity
to expose to media and others.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:34.110
So AIMS and the Next
Einstein Initiative

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:35.111
is doing really amazing.

00:11:35.111 --> 00:11:36.026
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:11:36.026 --> 00:11:36.660
Oh, please.

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:37.740
JESSICA PHALAFALA: I don't
know if you would also

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:39.380
like a student's
perspective, as well.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.260
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes, I would
like a student's perspective.

00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:43.593
JESSICA PHALAFALA: I would
say the one thing that really

00:11:43.593 --> 00:11:46.680
stands out about AIMS is that
it's research and learning

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:47.790
intensive.

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.332
I was at a university
for seven years,

00:11:50.332 --> 00:11:51.790
and the moment I
stepped into AIMS,

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.790
I can just feel the difference.

00:11:53.790 --> 00:11:55.350
It's a collaborative
environment.

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:56.820
Like you said, you
have researchers

00:11:56.820 --> 00:11:58.110
coming from across the world.

00:11:58.110 --> 00:12:01.530
And it gives you an opportunity
to network and interact

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:05.220
with people who are like-minded,
who can maybe share ideas

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:06.540
on the work that you're doing.

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.750
And also, it exposes
you to people

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.050
outside of the country that
are working in your area.

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:17.300
And especially when you are
exploring a new area like I am,

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:18.750
AIMS has given me
the opportunity

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:21.720
to do research visits in
Berlin and visit a team that's

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.880
working that has
experts in this field,

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.380
so that I'm exposed
to that, and I'm

00:12:25.380 --> 00:12:27.360
able to bring back those skills.

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:29.790
I think that's just the one
thing that really stands out

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.670
for me is that it's a
collaborative environment.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:35.560
It's an intensive
research-intensive environment.

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:38.640
And I think that makes it
different to the university

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:39.240
setup.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:39.870
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's wonderful.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.640
And you had mentioned
about in Berlin--

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.470
what other additional
collaborations are

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:45.240
done between AIMS
and other groups

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:47.190
that you haven't
already touched on?

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:49.770
How does AIMS participate
with other groups that

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.494
are within Africa,
as well as outside?

00:12:52.494 --> 00:12:53.910
YABEBAL FANTAYE:
There again, AIMS

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.760
is not-- so it gives
degrees, but it's actual

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:59.050
affiliated to
Stellenbosch University.

00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.560
So most of the
students do projects

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:04.470
in Stellenbosch University,
University of Cape Town,

00:13:04.470 --> 00:13:06.090
Wits, other places.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.490
So basically, it is not like--

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:12.330
it's an academic institute
but with wide-open door

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.130
for every collaboration
with different universities

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:16.200
within South Africa.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.180
And that is AIMS, South Africa.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.670
If it's AIMS, Ghana, it
would be like with all

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:22.440
the other universities locally.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:24.150
As well as,
internationally, people

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:27.630
would come to have some
research visits, grants

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:30.570
that people come and just
stay there for a month

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:34.140
to do some of their research,
and as Jessica was saying,

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:35.320
to collaborate.

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:37.350
That's where you
meet a lot of people,

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.990
not only just the people
from outside Africa,

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.460
but also from inside Africa.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:42.480
They come to teach.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.520
They come to research visit.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.140
And so those pursue
those affiliations

00:13:46.140 --> 00:13:47.520
to get those networks.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.430
So there's a lot
of this networking

00:13:50.430 --> 00:13:53.544
being done within Africa, as
well as also outside Africa.

00:13:53.544 --> 00:13:54.710
MELANIE WARRICK: Externally.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.694
And it's based on each
person's-- the work that

00:13:56.694 --> 00:13:58.860
they're doing, and how that
relates to other groups,

00:13:58.860 --> 00:13:59.634
as well.

00:13:59.634 --> 00:14:01.050
What additional
groups do you work

00:14:01.050 --> 00:14:03.190
with in terms of the
research that you're doing?

00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:06.270
YABEBAL FANTAYE: So for example,
the cosmology group at AIMS,

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:08.670
they have collaboration
with SKA, which

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:10.000
is the Square Kilometer Array.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.050
This is the biggest radio
telescope being built.

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.290
And we have the data
science group there,

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:19.380
and the data science group,
and the cosmology group at AIMS

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:20.220
are collaborating.

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:22.270
We work on similar projects.

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:25.620
And also every group has
also their own collaboration

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:26.690
that they bring together.

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:28.920
For example, I have
collaboration in Munich,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:31.350
and have collaboration
in Rome, and Oslo.

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:35.070
Which I basically-- through
that I bring visitors,

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.130
and I also go and visit.

00:14:37.130 --> 00:14:39.610
So it's the same, I think,
as Jessica was saying,

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.670
there is a lot of these
collaborations going on.

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.253
MELANIE WARRICK:
Sharing of knowledge.

00:14:43.253 --> 00:14:44.040
This is great.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.320
And Jessica, you had
mentioned you were at Wits.

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:47.780
What is Wits?

00:14:47.780 --> 00:14:49.470
JESSICA PHALAFALA:
So the full name

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:51.840
is the University of
the Witwatersrand.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.120
It's in Johannesburg.

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:57.600
And it's one of our oldest
universities in South Africa

00:14:57.600 --> 00:15:00.480
and ranked the top
in South Africa.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:02.450
I did my undergrad
through to my master's.

00:15:02.450 --> 00:15:03.991
MELANIE WARRICK:
That's good to know.

00:15:03.991 --> 00:15:06.030
I mean, I know most
universities do research.

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:08.030
Do they have a very
established research program

00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:10.100
as well, affiliations
with other groups as well?

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:11.280
Because it sounds
like there's work

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.988
that's being done with
AIMS between Wits.

00:15:12.988 --> 00:15:15.330
And I've heard of
it many times now.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:17.550
JESSICA PHALAFALA: Yes,
so I think this is--

00:15:17.550 --> 00:15:19.440
I don't know about
in other fields,

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.440
I think data science are
more informed, because that's

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:25.200
where it directly affects me.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:28.620
We have a German research
chair at AIMS who's actually

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:32.400
working with some of
the supervisors at Wits,

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:34.200
and they are trying
to also create

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:37.900
a community of data scientists
who can collaborate together.

00:15:37.900 --> 00:15:40.590
So one example of
this is there's

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:44.130
a four-year course called the
Theoretical Foundations of Data

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:48.300
Science that was started by the
former vice chancellor of Wits,

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.370
and Bubacarr Bah,
who is at AIMS.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:54.870
And what they are doing is
taking staff members who

00:15:54.870 --> 00:15:57.390
have master's degrees
and introducing them

00:15:57.390 --> 00:16:01.110
to data science so that they
can explore it for their PhD.

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.850
So just initiatives like
that are taking place.

00:16:03.850 --> 00:16:07.170
So I do think that
it is taking part

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:10.740
in creating programs for data
science and other research

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:11.550
areas.

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:12.690
That's just one example.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:13.722
I'm sure there are many.

00:16:13.722 --> 00:16:15.430
YABEBAL FANTAYE:
Physics sector, as well.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:18.600
They have very strong particle
physics research group,

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.010
string theory, they're
very well known.

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:22.920
So there are many
of these research

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.250
groups that are strong at Wits.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.250
MELANIE WARRICK: And it's
very well established.

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:29.040
I know another group
that I've heard

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.830
of talk a little bit
about this is CSIR.

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:35.409
Can you tell us a little bit
about CSIR, that you know of?

00:16:35.409 --> 00:16:37.200
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Yes,
the only thing I know

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:40.580
is that they are involved
in many sectors of research.

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:43.090
So for example, I
know them through--

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:46.530
they manage, as far as I know,
the center for high performance

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:49.350
computing, which is
based in Cape Town.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.210
This is going to be
also the one machine

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.700
that SK is going to
be using, because they

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.150
have the experience, as well
as just the machines to do it.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:17:00.210
And my master's student has
been, for example, using that

00:17:00.210 --> 00:17:01.880
just for his own research.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.859
So they provide the
high-performance computing

00:17:04.859 --> 00:17:05.550
platform.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:09.339
And so they are involved in
many, many other research.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:12.180
For example, we got
our satellite data

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.060
from the SANSA, which is
the National Space Science

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:19.800
Agency here, and they are also
affiliated to CSIR in some way.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.790
In every branch I go,
I actually see them--

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:24.720
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:28.450
So it's kind of adding
one of the main groups.

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:30.270
It incorporates many
research groups.

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:30.810
MELANIE WARRICK:
It sounds like it's

00:17:30.810 --> 00:17:33.090
based in multiple
locations, similar to AIMS.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.990
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Because there
is one in Cape Town I know.

00:17:34.990 --> 00:17:36.656
JESSICA PHALAFALA:
And Pretoria as well.

00:17:36.656 --> 00:17:38.910
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, what
about some of the smaller

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:40.537
affiliated groups
that you work with?

00:17:40.537 --> 00:17:42.120
What are some of the
examples of those

00:17:42.120 --> 00:17:44.578
that are-- they might not be
located in multiple locations,

00:17:44.578 --> 00:17:46.572
but they do specific
and very deep research?

00:17:46.572 --> 00:17:48.780
I know you've talked a little
bit about some of them.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.490
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Yeah, there
are many such initiatives.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:53.880
For example, I came
out from the program

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:56.765
called the National
Astrophysics and Space Science--

00:17:56.765 --> 00:18:00.220
NASS-- the National Astrophysics
and Space Science program.

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.870
And when I came to do
my honors and master's

00:18:02.870 --> 00:18:06.450
through that program, it has
trained in the space science

00:18:06.450 --> 00:18:08.610
and astrophysics sector a lot.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:10.740
I would say it has
really influenced it--

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:12.300
the landscape in Africa--

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.040
in terms of like,
graduate now, you'd

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:17.910
find also graduates from
many countries in that field.

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:19.770
And that is a
collaboration I gained

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:23.070
between almost every
university in South Africa.

00:18:23.070 --> 00:18:26.070
So it's like we
were collaborating

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:29.390
with [INAUDIBLE] Observatory,
the observatory in Cape Town,

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:31.290
satellites, all
this collaboration.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.870
But every university, for
example, in the Western Cape,

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:36.370
they have very strong
cosmology group.

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:37.890
They are doing
research, and they

00:18:37.890 --> 00:18:40.410
are one of the main
science drivers

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.590
now in terms of research,
I would say, for the SK.

00:18:43.590 --> 00:18:45.870
They have lots of
stuff doing in SK.

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:49.170
And as I said, in Wits,
UCT has many groups.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:52.022
The cosmology group at UCT
was the strongest at the time.

00:18:52.022 --> 00:18:52.730
I don't know now.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:55.980
But it's like they had the
string theory, the cosmology,

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.920
which was led by
George Ellis, who

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:01.140
was a very prominent
cosmologist, who worked also

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:05.250
with other very prominent
scientists in the field.

00:19:05.250 --> 00:19:07.100
So there are a lot--

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:09.030
the astronomy
department, if you go,

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.310
they have research,
big research group.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:14.070
Even those that I'm aware
that I've given a talk

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:17.100
there, or I have
worked with people,

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:18.759
there are a lot of
research groups.

00:19:18.759 --> 00:19:21.300
MELANIE WARRICK: I want to touch
on this-- so we're recording

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:24.017
this, and I didn't start out the
conversation with this point,

00:19:24.017 --> 00:19:26.100
but we are recording this
at Deep Learning Indaba.

00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:28.080
I've been emphasizing
that a lot when I

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:30.310
talk about this on our podcast.

00:19:30.310 --> 00:19:33.450
We just were actually, before
we recorded this at a session,

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:35.880
where we were listening
to a couple of people talk

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.330
about democratizing
AI in Africa.

00:19:38.330 --> 00:19:40.950
One of the points that
Nandu de Freitas made

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:44.700
was that we need to have
AI academic conferences,

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:48.360
like, in Africa, like
AI academic conferences,

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.690
a large one, that's
established here.

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:52.290
And from everything
you're telling me,

00:19:52.290 --> 00:19:54.420
there's significant
amount of research.

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:56.550
Now, we haven't
touched specifically

00:19:56.550 --> 00:19:58.650
around the data science
in the AI space,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.690
but there is a significant
amount of research

00:20:00.690 --> 00:20:01.910
that's going on.

00:20:01.910 --> 00:20:04.050
What are your thoughts
on his perspective?

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.030
JESSICA PHALAFALA: I
definitely agree with him.

00:20:06.030 --> 00:20:07.076
I have to agree with him.

00:20:07.076 --> 00:20:09.450
Especially as someone who's
just moved into data science,

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:11.790
I see the importance
of events like this.

00:20:11.790 --> 00:20:15.990
Research can sometimes
be an isolating thing

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:17.680
to do and to take on.

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:21.340
But when you are brought
into a space like this one,

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:24.550
you get an opportunity to
really put your ideas out

00:20:24.550 --> 00:20:26.710
there and have people
share their opinions

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:27.670
and give you feedback.

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:30.460
And I think feedback is
really important, especially

00:20:30.460 --> 00:20:34.852
in a field that is still
developing at the rate

00:20:34.852 --> 00:20:37.060
that data science and machine
learning is developing.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:39.310
You really want to be
in a space where you're

00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:41.190
producing work that's relevant.

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:43.090
And you only know if
your work is relevant

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:46.970
if you interact with
other people in that area.

00:20:46.970 --> 00:20:51.250
So this is really something I
wish would take place, just not

00:20:51.250 --> 00:20:52.930
the Deep Learning Indaba.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.560
I wish there would be more
conferences of this nature.

00:20:55.560 --> 00:20:57.259
Yeah, really, it's
an opportunity

00:20:57.259 --> 00:20:59.050
to put your name out
there as a researcher,

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:00.980
it's an opportunity to
interact with others.

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.620
It's an opportunity
to get feedback,

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:04.370
to collaborate with others.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.300
And we need to be at a stage
where we can collaborate

00:21:07.300 --> 00:21:09.610
across countries in Africa.

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:11.590
And I think this
is the best place

00:21:11.590 --> 00:21:14.170
to meet those people
in other countries that

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:16.710
are working on something similar
to what you're working on.

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:17.830
YABEBAL FANTAYE:
Yeah, I think it's--

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:18.940
to add on that, also--

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:21.040
I think it's the
most important aspect

00:21:21.040 --> 00:21:23.710
is that usually these
are signs that people

00:21:23.710 --> 00:21:27.610
are taking it serious and
people are paying attention.

00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:30.580
Governments then would respond
to it and policies would--

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:34.240
so this is just like
a sign that also

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:37.750
shows you like, OK, people have
understood the significance.

00:21:37.750 --> 00:21:40.570
And the bigger it
is, it also tells you

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:42.330
people are active in the field.

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:45.430
They're trying to work
on that and creating

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:47.470
that platform, basically.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:49.090
I mean, I have seen
lots of research

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:51.820
came out from Sudan
and other places

00:21:51.820 --> 00:21:54.940
where there are no
actual staff doing it,

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:56.650
but the students are interested.

00:21:56.650 --> 00:21:59.530
And so therefore, they kind
of have this supervisor,

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:02.290
but who's also probably
not in the field,

00:22:02.290 --> 00:22:04.660
but they go and do it,
the ones I observed.

00:22:04.660 --> 00:22:09.650
It's such a nice, amazing
research, the idea is nice.

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:13.360
The other one that impressed
me was this Sudanese girl.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:16.225
She showed me the
statistics like, OK,

00:22:16.225 --> 00:22:19.420
there is depression in the
Arabic world-- many people die

00:22:19.420 --> 00:22:20.690
out of suicide and depression.

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:22.310
MELANIE WARRICK: From the
mental health standpoint, yeah.

00:22:22.310 --> 00:22:24.010
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Yes, in Sudan.

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:26.410
And so she thought,
oh, you know, why not?

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:27.247
Can we help them?

00:22:27.247 --> 00:22:29.080
And then also, of course,
she showed me also

00:22:29.080 --> 00:22:31.600
some of the statistics
she got from the,

00:22:31.600 --> 00:22:33.580
I think, the UN
organization, where

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:36.550
it shows young people who
are depressed actually

00:22:36.550 --> 00:22:39.610
go to social media
to say something

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:41.230
about their depression.

00:22:41.230 --> 00:22:43.780
And so her idea was
like, oh, can we then

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:47.869
discover that early on such
that they can actually get help?

00:22:47.869 --> 00:22:49.660
And of course, the
method that she implied,

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:52.210
she collected by hand,
because she said in Sudan,

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.850
many of the social
media are not working.

00:22:54.850 --> 00:22:56.800
So she collected by
hand from Facebook.

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.660
You know, she went
to join the groups,

00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:03.310
and then just collected this
data by hand, labeled by hand.

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:05.800
She got about 3,000
from Twitter and stuff.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:08.510
Of course, there were some
flaws, and in her derivations,

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:09.610
but that's it.

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:12.310
The point is that she
has got everything,

00:23:12.310 --> 00:23:14.770
but there was no one
probably to guide her,

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:17.360
so like all the
technical difficulties.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:20.080
And coming here into the
Deep Learning Indaba,

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:22.120
and just me being
there, and at least

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:24.190
saying, like, OK, this
is an amazing thing,

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:26.950
but you have these sampling
issues, these things.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:29.130
It just would give
her that insight,

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:31.360
and then she can
form collaborations.

00:23:31.360 --> 00:23:32.860
So all these things
wouldn't have

00:23:32.860 --> 00:23:38.350
happened if you don't have such
exposure in venues to present

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:40.060
your work, however small it is.

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:42.430
But putting your effort
out so that people

00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:44.620
can then give you
the edge to just go

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:46.900
over and be what you can be.

00:23:46.900 --> 00:23:49.210
And so I think this
Deep Learning Indaba,

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:51.580
and similarly there
is also Indaba-X,

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:55.090
but it's really a great start,
and there should be more.

00:23:55.090 --> 00:23:59.110
And for me, there was a
depressing statistic recently

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:00.880
that came out from
McKinsey that it's

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:02.680
going to be this revolution.

00:24:02.680 --> 00:24:07.630
AI revolution is going to
be changing the GDP as more

00:24:07.630 --> 00:24:09.880
or less in the same
level as the one which

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:11.830
is the first
Industrial Revolution--

00:24:11.830 --> 00:24:13.450
which we know, they affect.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.630
It just created a massive shift.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.580
But their simulation
shows that actually

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:20.410
Africa and other
developing countries

00:24:20.410 --> 00:24:22.060
may not get the benefit.

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:25.480
Because already the landscape
has been so much shifted

00:24:25.480 --> 00:24:29.680
to mostly US and China and other
countries which are already

00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:30.400
strong.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:33.730
So you really need some
kind of intervention

00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:36.530
to make this prediction wrong.

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:38.750
Because we want to
be participating.

00:24:38.750 --> 00:24:40.170
We want to benefit from it.

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:40.920
We want to change.

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:44.170
And other, like the WEF studies,
for example, the World Economic

00:24:44.170 --> 00:24:46.540
Forum, they actually put the
food Industrial Revolution

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:48.040
to be maximal--

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:50.510
Africa to be maximally
benefiting from those.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:52.750
So there are these
predictions where Africa

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:56.230
has all the challenges that
can be addressed, most of them

00:24:56.230 --> 00:24:58.900
can be addressed through
systematization, resource

00:24:58.900 --> 00:25:00.292
management, and all that.

00:25:00.292 --> 00:25:01.750
And at the same
time, here you have

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:04.390
the economic, the GDP thing.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:07.810
So if you match them, there is--
if these platforms are becoming

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:08.650
more and more--

00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:11.104
I definitely think those
simulations can be wrong.

00:25:11.104 --> 00:25:12.520
MELANIE WARRICK:
What do you think

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:15.250
could be done, from a research
institution standpoint,

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:17.290
to help this GDP issue?

00:25:17.290 --> 00:25:19.960
The fact that there is this
expectation that you're already

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:21.154
behind the times, I guess?

00:25:21.154 --> 00:25:22.570
JESSICA PHALAFALA:
I think what is

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:27.700
important is for us to focus
on African problems in terms

00:25:27.700 --> 00:25:31.180
of research and not try and
produce research that is not

00:25:31.180 --> 00:25:33.920
related to solving
African problems--

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:36.730
whether it be the drought
in Cape Town, et cetera.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:42.220
That would keep our research
relevant to the African climate

00:25:42.220 --> 00:25:45.490
and also allow the results of
our research to benefit us.

00:25:45.490 --> 00:25:48.520
And maybe that's when we will
see the developments in AI

00:25:48.520 --> 00:25:52.770
also benefit our GDP,
and maybe as a country,

00:25:52.770 --> 00:25:54.610
as African states.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:57.820
Another thing I think
is research institutes

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:01.570
need to do their very best
to keep their students

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:05.650
and not lose them
to universities

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:09.160
in North America, and
the UK, and Europe,

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:11.380
as the case has
been for many years.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:14.920
This causes us to fall
behind, in terms of research.

00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:19.360
This also causes us to not have
ownership of African research.

00:26:19.360 --> 00:26:20.860
For me, these are
the kind of things

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:23.530
that are hindering the
progress with respect

00:26:23.530 --> 00:26:25.810
to research in AI and
machine learning--

00:26:25.810 --> 00:26:29.290
holding on to researchers
and also working

00:26:29.290 --> 00:26:31.450
on African problems.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.700
YABEBAL FANTAYE: I think
another aspect, which

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.070
I would say, for
example, another research

00:26:36.070 --> 00:26:38.950
that I was really greatly
enjoyed in the post-doc session

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:42.130
was Jessica's, who
was working actually

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:45.220
on the foundations
of Deep Learning.

00:26:45.220 --> 00:26:48.070
I believe that it's just
not only you have to--

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:50.220
and you have to
build the confidence,

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:52.600
not just only to do
something, but also

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:54.820
to follow your curiosity.

00:26:54.820 --> 00:26:57.730
So I think what they are doing,
for example, in this Manifold

00:26:57.730 --> 00:27:00.490
Learning, trying to find,
ascertain aspects, such

00:27:00.490 --> 00:27:04.232
that they can then
improve and understand

00:27:04.232 --> 00:27:05.315
how the actual black box--

00:27:05.315 --> 00:27:07.510
JESSICA PHALAFALA: The
mathematical foundations of--

00:27:07.510 --> 00:27:09.135
YABEBAL FANTAYE: Yes,
how the black box

00:27:09.135 --> 00:27:12.580
is working, and trying to
then get the-- so competing

00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:14.570
at global, at the cutting edge.

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:17.020
And that's also, you need
that kind of confidence

00:27:17.020 --> 00:27:18.120
and you need to build it.

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.230
Because without that
confidence, it's

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:21.760
basically going
to be like, we're

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:24.710
just going to happy
to be sitting nearby

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:25.887
in somebody, right?

00:27:25.887 --> 00:27:28.470
And I think that mentality-- and
that's why I really like also

00:27:28.470 --> 00:27:30.490
Neil Turkos' way of
thinking sometimes,

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:33.730
and why there is the Quantum
Leap Africa Institute, which

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:35.560
we just started recently.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:37.780
And the whole idea is
like, yeah, let's be bold.

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:39.800
Because in this
world, as I think

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:41.620
they say it again
and again, it's

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:43.750
only for those who
really can dream big

00:27:43.750 --> 00:27:45.260
and just are not afraid--

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:46.990
MELANIE WARRICK:
And take the risks.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.960
YABEBAL FANTAYE: So that is
like in the Quantum, we know--

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:54.730
right now, it's still the game
is not decided in data science

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:55.940
and in AI.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:58.270
We have a lot to contribute.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:01.280
But we also have to plan the
future, to lead, for example,

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:04.150
some of the expected
innovations or revolutions

00:28:04.150 --> 00:28:06.020
that may be coming
with quantum computing.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:07.478
So the Quantum Leap
Africa is like,

00:28:07.478 --> 00:28:09.580
OK, let's gear our
mind that we will

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:11.650
be the leaders in that field.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:13.810
Right now we have data science.

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:17.490
Let's build-- because it
requires the computer.

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.530
The quantum machine
will require algorithms,

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:21.730
will require sensors, and stuff.

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:24.400
Let's build to what
we have now to that.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:26.080
Let's have all the expertise.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:28.680
Let's not be worrying about
whether it's this or that.

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:30.730
It's like, let's
build our confidence.

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:33.250
Let's have that
kind of ambition.

00:28:33.250 --> 00:28:34.610
Because it's only the ambition.

00:28:34.610 --> 00:28:36.580
And I think I can
share so many times

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:40.580
that the people in West Africa,
when they play football,

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:42.580
it is their ambition
that drives them--

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:45.490
that makes them play in Europe.

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:48.820
And people in Kenya and in
East Africa, in general,

00:28:48.820 --> 00:28:50.680
when they run--
which is just also

00:28:50.680 --> 00:28:52.450
partially-- it influences me.

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:55.380
I just think about winning.

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:57.220
I just even don't think about--

00:28:57.220 --> 00:28:59.670
that confidence was
built by few people

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:02.680
who broke just stats,
that kind of sphere where

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:03.790
it was blocking.

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:05.782
And suddenly someone
just got gold,

00:29:05.782 --> 00:29:07.240
or they were just
the best players,

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:09.900
and then people were
like, it was me.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.500
I can relate my life.

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.090
There was nothing special.

00:29:13.090 --> 00:29:15.520
And so it was just you
build that confidence.

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:17.920
And so all these
ambitious projects,

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:20.800
as well as I am also leading
the community of scientists

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:25.120
in Africa, which incorporates
the Next Einstein Fellows,

00:29:25.120 --> 00:29:28.080
and Next Einstein Ambassadors
from all over Africa,

00:29:28.080 --> 00:29:30.160
and we are trying
also exactly to do

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.440
like, we are doing
something, we are

00:29:32.440 --> 00:29:34.990
close to the research
and the knowledge.

00:29:34.990 --> 00:29:37.880
And the politicians are
very close to the decisions

00:29:37.880 --> 00:29:39.060
they have to do every day.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:39.850
It's a hard job.

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:42.460
The business people have to
deal and they have to survive.

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:44.770
So that dialogue we can form.

00:29:44.770 --> 00:29:46.197
Because we have
the time to think,

00:29:46.197 --> 00:29:47.530
and we have the time to reflect.

00:29:47.530 --> 00:29:48.940
We are easy to the data.

00:29:48.940 --> 00:29:51.320
We can analyze and
get the insights.

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:53.060
And then we have
to share that one.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:55.360
So we have in this
policy advising--

00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:59.260
so we have objectives
to try to be advising AU

00:29:59.260 --> 00:30:02.770
in some, because these fellows
and ambassadors are working

00:30:02.770 --> 00:30:07.060
on every field, from medical
to climate to economics

00:30:07.060 --> 00:30:09.610
to computer science,
data science.

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:10.530
So it's everywhere.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:11.576
We have all those fields.

00:30:11.576 --> 00:30:13.200
And we are increasing--
we are not just

00:30:13.200 --> 00:30:14.300
fellow or an ambassador.

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:16.564
It's all about let's
build the community,

00:30:16.564 --> 00:30:19.650
and let this community
contribute in all forms.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:21.490
Just let it not to be doormat--

00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:23.480
the whole point is,
let us not be passive.

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:27.040
And in this transformation, all
the work, the fundamental work,

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.940
the pure mathematician,
has a lot to contribute,

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.760
as well as the one
who is doing just

00:30:32.760 --> 00:30:35.710
basically in the clinical
testing in some remote places.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:37.320
So there is a lot more to do.

00:30:37.320 --> 00:30:39.360
So I think those
would, I would say,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:42.420
contribute in making
sure if we keep

00:30:42.420 --> 00:30:44.010
doing them in the right way.

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:47.820
I think it will make these
predictions wrong, I would say.

00:30:47.820 --> 00:30:49.500
MELANIE WARRICK:
And I noticed when

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:51.600
I've been talking to
people at the conference

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:55.380
that there is a common fear
that I don't know enough.

00:30:55.380 --> 00:30:56.670
I'm afraid to jump in.

00:30:56.670 --> 00:30:58.980
But yet I'm here
because I'm interested.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:00.570
And I'm bringing
this up because I've

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:02.920
talked to a lot of people
who are in this space,

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:05.461
or who want to be in this
space, in many locations--

00:31:05.461 --> 00:31:06.960
I've been very lucky
in that regard,

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:09.330
to be able to talk
to so many people who

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:12.090
are trying to get into AI
from all over the world.

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:15.354
And there's that common fear
and common intimidation--

00:31:15.354 --> 00:31:16.020
it's everywhere.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:17.190
And it's here.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:20.530
And I've heard people talking
about this here, as well.

00:31:20.530 --> 00:31:22.480
So you're talking
about the confidence.

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:24.390
I think that the
question becomes

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:27.684
how to encourage people who
don't feel like they know.

00:31:27.684 --> 00:31:29.100
I think that's
also the key, too--

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:32.290
don't feel like they know
enough, and they're afraid.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:33.372
How to encourage them.

00:31:33.372 --> 00:31:34.830
And what do you
think could be done

00:31:34.830 --> 00:31:37.110
to help make sure that
the barriers to entry

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:39.882
don't feel as intimidating?

00:31:39.882 --> 00:31:41.340
JESSICA PHALAFALA:
Firstly, I think

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:43.020
one of your first
questions was, why

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:46.420
do people feel intimidated
by this area of study?

00:31:46.420 --> 00:31:49.220
And I think from what
I've seen thus far

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:52.350
is that machine
learning or data science

00:31:52.350 --> 00:31:55.140
is a multidisciplinary area.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.840
And I think that's the
intimidating part--

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:00.870
that mathematics
alone is not enough.

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.420
It's a combination of
statistics, mathematics,

00:32:03.420 --> 00:32:04.920
and computer science.

00:32:04.920 --> 00:32:06.777
And that requires--
if you're coming

00:32:06.777 --> 00:32:08.360
from any of those
angles, you're going

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:12.430
to be required to broaden your
knowledge on the other topics.

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:14.640
So I think that's
the first thing.

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:16.830
And I don't think
it's something that

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:21.600
should be seen as a barrier,
because the support is there--

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.650
especially in universities.

00:32:23.650 --> 00:32:26.010
Especially because this
is an upcoming field.

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:30.180
So supervisors, institutes,
are aware that people

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:31.800
who are doing
research in this area

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:34.380
require a little bit more
support in that regard.

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.840
I think conferences
such as this one

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:39.690
do help provide you
with that confidence,

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:41.490
like Yabebal was saying.

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:43.500
I'm working on a
problem, and being

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:46.470
able to present to
my peers, and present

00:32:46.470 --> 00:32:50.280
to people who are more
experienced in this field, also

00:32:50.280 --> 00:32:52.710
allows me to gain
more knowledge,

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:56.280
and allows them to also nudge
me in the right direction,

00:32:56.280 --> 00:32:58.100
provide me with the
resources that I need.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:00.390
So these kind of
conferences are really--

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:03.480
it's really important to have
these kind of engagements,

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:05.662
where when you
present, someone might

00:33:05.662 --> 00:33:07.620
be a little bit more
experienced in that field,

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.120
and you're stuck
on something that,

00:33:09.120 --> 00:33:12.270
for them, is just a quick,
hey, here's a resource.

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:13.750
Once you've taken
a look at this,

00:33:13.750 --> 00:33:15.450
it will push you in
the right direction.

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:17.850
Or hey, here's my email
address, let's meet up

00:33:17.850 --> 00:33:19.780
and we'll take this further.

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.200
So I think that's a
good starting point.

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:24.220
There are many other
things that can be done.

00:33:24.220 --> 00:33:27.630
But this, for me, is, in the
year that I've been studying,

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:30.720
this has been one of those
moments where I've learned

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:32.520
so much in a period
of four days--

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:35.190
it hasn't even come
to an end yet--

00:33:35.190 --> 00:33:38.179
I think that that really
does get the job done.

00:33:38.179 --> 00:33:39.720
YABEBAL FANTAYE:
Just to add on that,

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:41.910
I think she said most
of the things also

00:33:41.910 --> 00:33:44.880
I would have liked to say in
terms of this kind of platforms

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:47.010
definitely put you
in that context.

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:51.740
The people who are doing great
out there and the differences

00:33:51.740 --> 00:33:53.440
are not that big as you think.

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:55.786
And so it just gives you
the proof to yourself

00:33:55.786 --> 00:33:57.660
to check that you are
in the right direction,

00:33:57.660 --> 00:34:00.300
or this is the things you miss,
or the things you thought--

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:02.250
it's big, it's not big.

00:34:02.250 --> 00:34:05.460
It gives you the laboratory
to check for yourself.

00:34:05.460 --> 00:34:07.740
And usually those, I
believe, are the only way

00:34:07.740 --> 00:34:09.540
that you will get confidence.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:12.270
So another aspect that I am
working on that, because that's

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:15.580
for me the most important
thing that happened to my life,

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:17.670
so I relate directly
to it, is that

00:34:17.670 --> 00:34:20.400
because, you could be planning--

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:21.739
I was ambitious.

00:34:21.739 --> 00:34:25.164
Abebe Bikila for me was
like, yeah, what did he do?

00:34:25.164 --> 00:34:27.080
Abebe Bikila is one of
the runners in Ethiopia

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:32.520
who got the first Olympic
gold medal in the 1960s--

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:35.219
he was the first African
to actually do that.

00:34:35.219 --> 00:34:39.336
And it inspired a generation
of runners from then on.

00:34:39.336 --> 00:34:40.710
And there were a
couple of things

00:34:40.710 --> 00:34:43.469
he did, which I don't know
how that relates, that fits,

00:34:43.469 --> 00:34:45.120
but for me, I like
the narratives

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:46.590
because it really helps me.

00:34:46.590 --> 00:34:48.960
He was running
barefoot, and he was

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:50.440
training in Ethiopia barefoot.

00:34:50.440 --> 00:34:53.380
And he went to Japan
in the Tokyo marathon,

00:34:53.380 --> 00:34:55.880
then he was given
a shoe to run to.

00:34:55.880 --> 00:34:57.900
And he had to decide--

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.830
that's my narration,
I like to put it--

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:02.970
and he had to decide
to run on this thing

00:35:02.970 --> 00:35:06.780
that he hasn't got used
to, everyone is doing it.

00:35:06.780 --> 00:35:10.470
Or just do his way,
the way he knows.

00:35:10.470 --> 00:35:13.740
And I think, for me, the
most spectacular event

00:35:13.740 --> 00:35:17.900
was he decided to just run
barefoot-- just his own way.

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:22.140
And then, he ran, just
completely made history.

00:35:22.140 --> 00:35:25.250
So I think one thing I
draw from that experience,

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:26.720
and the same can
be said, I think,

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:29.210
in the Western Africa
for the football,

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:32.510
probably, is people,
to achieve greatness,

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:35.330
they didn't just
have to pretend--

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:37.490
they had to do their own way.

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:40.280
And they had to be
taking the consequences.

00:35:40.280 --> 00:35:42.550
So for us, I took that
inspiration for me,

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.990
and we have this
project which is,

00:35:44.990 --> 00:35:47.437
I would say, a significant
fraction of my three years,

00:35:47.437 --> 00:35:49.520
in the last three years I
spent thinking about it,

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:52.190
because that's
what's important--

00:35:52.190 --> 00:35:56.380
checking every detail and
how you moved in this space.

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.570
And what we do that
it's actually--

00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:01.460
it's influenced whom you
knew when you were a child.

00:36:01.460 --> 00:36:03.350
Did you know a mathematician?

00:36:03.350 --> 00:36:05.720
And if not, when you
become a mathematician,

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:09.110
you won't be confident, because
you feel like you may not

00:36:09.110 --> 00:36:10.624
understanding it the right way.

00:36:10.624 --> 00:36:13.040
If you didn't know a physicist,
when you got into physics,

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:13.970
you're not sure.

00:36:13.970 --> 00:36:17.120
But it just seems
so naturally so nice

00:36:17.120 --> 00:36:21.110
is that, even just one handshake
when you were probably four

00:36:21.110 --> 00:36:23.480
years old, with a
physicist, could

00:36:23.480 --> 00:36:25.190
have changed that thing a lot.

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:27.900
Because then, that
propagates in time--

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:30.020
like when you talk
with your peers,

00:36:30.020 --> 00:36:32.330
you probably would say,
yeah, I know a mathematician,

00:36:32.330 --> 00:36:34.270
they do this.

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:37.460
But then you think, every
time you say those statements,

00:36:37.460 --> 00:36:39.290
you're actually thinking.

00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.290
So it just doesn't even
need sometimes maybe a lot,

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:45.590
it just needs people
everywhere in remote areas,

00:36:45.590 --> 00:36:49.310
they need to see the real people
doing that sort of modeling.

00:36:49.310 --> 00:36:53.210
So what we are doing, we have
this project which is really,

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:57.380
as I said, the significant
part of my thinking

00:36:57.380 --> 00:36:59.330
in the last three
years spent on that,

00:36:59.330 --> 00:37:01.469
is that we have
Astrobus Ethiopia.

00:37:01.469 --> 00:37:03.260
So what we do is that
we're taking the bus,

00:37:03.260 --> 00:37:04.490
we get all the people--

00:37:04.490 --> 00:37:07.280
and we don't distinguish,
because abstraction

00:37:07.280 --> 00:37:10.760
and composition is an
idea, you abstract it,

00:37:10.760 --> 00:37:13.070
and then use those
abstracts to compose.

00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:14.720
That is mathematics,
that is physics,

00:37:14.720 --> 00:37:17.840
that is art, that is fashion
design, that is painting,

00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.210
that poem, that is everything.

00:37:20.210 --> 00:37:22.700
You get to some
abstract level, you

00:37:22.700 --> 00:37:26.090
use that abstract level to
create some symphony-- just

00:37:26.090 --> 00:37:26.630
like music.

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:28.280
Notes to symphonies.

00:37:28.280 --> 00:37:30.240
And so we take that one.

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:31.551
So we bring everyone--

00:37:31.551 --> 00:37:33.050
all those people
who believed in it,

00:37:33.050 --> 00:37:36.850
but who are also doing things,
like real fashion designers,

00:37:36.850 --> 00:37:37.670
if it's scientists.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:40.460
Scientists really have
that ambition and to do.

00:37:40.460 --> 00:37:44.400
Inventors-- so we have art,
science, and innovation.

00:37:44.400 --> 00:37:45.950
We put together,
we put in the bus,

00:37:45.950 --> 00:37:48.600
and we just drive across
the whole country.

00:37:48.600 --> 00:37:50.780
And we just get
off, and what we do

00:37:50.780 --> 00:37:53.180
is mostly not teaching--
it's not about teaching.

00:37:53.180 --> 00:37:56.540
It's about belongingness-- you
belong to me, I belong to you.

00:37:56.540 --> 00:37:58.010
We are in the same family.

00:37:58.010 --> 00:38:00.770
So when you know me, and when
you see me now in the TV,

00:38:00.770 --> 00:38:02.955
you say, I know that guy--

00:38:02.955 --> 00:38:04.030
I met him.

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:07.340
And that confidence, we
take that inspiration,

00:38:07.340 --> 00:38:10.250
and we are doing it, and we
are trying to expand it also

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:11.180
in other parts.

00:38:11.180 --> 00:38:13.250
We wanted, for
example, in Rwanda--

00:38:13.250 --> 00:38:15.020
I want Rwandese to do it.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:17.210
In Senegal, I want the
Senegalese to do it.

00:38:17.210 --> 00:38:20.500
Because that relation cuts
that barrier which like,

00:38:20.500 --> 00:38:23.570
you may have a
different opportunity.

00:38:23.570 --> 00:38:26.360
So in Ethiopia, we want
to do it with Ethiopians.

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:27.740
We're doing in IBM.

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:29.750
We're doing it in
Silicon Valley.

00:38:29.750 --> 00:38:32.630
We're doing research in
cosmology and fashion design--

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:34.490
we want them to be
there and just shake,

00:38:34.490 --> 00:38:38.060
because then the kids don't
have any more reasoning

00:38:38.060 --> 00:38:41.570
to say they are different.

00:38:41.570 --> 00:38:43.070
Because they are them.

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:45.050
MELANIE WARRICK: I love the fact
that there are many people here

00:38:45.050 --> 00:38:47.675
who have come to this conference
who are very much junior, very

00:38:47.675 --> 00:38:52.010
much just entering this space,
who have taken the risk,

00:38:52.010 --> 00:38:54.320
have overcome
their intimidation.

00:38:54.320 --> 00:38:57.350
And also, I've seen so many
people who welcomed them in,

00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:59.120
and been very inclusive.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:00.860
And that's been
really impressive.

00:39:00.860 --> 00:39:04.490
Before we wrap up, I just want
to touch on a couple of things.

00:39:04.490 --> 00:39:07.370
Any other things
that you've seen

00:39:07.370 --> 00:39:09.980
that you were really excited
about by this conference,

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:11.420
that you wanted to touch on?

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:12.800
That you wanted to mention?

00:39:12.800 --> 00:39:14.120
YABEBAL FANTAYE: I think
there are, as I say,

00:39:14.120 --> 00:39:15.170
I mentioned some of them.

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:18.770
I really was-- even if Jessica
is at AIMS, and we talked,

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:20.830
and I know her supervisor--

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:23.650
it was very good to see
what they are doing.

00:39:23.650 --> 00:39:26.600
And so I went to
her poster, and I

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:30.570
asked her to tell me in
detail what's going on.

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:34.550
So it was really impressive to
see that confidence of really

00:39:34.550 --> 00:39:38.270
working on just the foundation
of deep learning, especially

00:39:38.270 --> 00:39:41.270
this black box that we
still don't understand.

00:39:41.270 --> 00:39:43.220
Trying to find--
and there method

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.920
is different, and
clever, and I am not

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:47.060
qualified to say anything.

00:39:47.060 --> 00:39:51.380
But it's really impressive that
people are thinking and pushing

00:39:51.380 --> 00:39:54.110
in the boundary-- not only
just in the application side,

00:39:54.110 --> 00:39:56.610
but also just in
the pure math side.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:59.990
And another aspect is just those
people, that is one example,

00:39:59.990 --> 00:40:03.290
but I have seen other people
doing really clever, but more

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:07.530
concerned from their own
they think is a problem.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:11.090
So then they're trying to
use the methods available,

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:13.190
and trying to improve life.

00:40:13.190 --> 00:40:15.890
So I think those are
the moments I was

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:18.240
very much happy that I am here.

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:22.280
And even if I am not a
big believer in this big--

00:40:22.280 --> 00:40:24.900
big conferences are
like we draw ourselves,

00:40:24.900 --> 00:40:26.830
and we just look and we meet.

00:40:26.830 --> 00:40:29.810
Networking is the biggest thing
for me in these kind of things.

00:40:29.810 --> 00:40:32.400
And as I say, me
talking to that person,

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:35.440
the other person talking, and
just guiding here and there

00:40:35.440 --> 00:40:37.900
and other person guiding
me on the problems

00:40:37.900 --> 00:40:40.660
they have-- so these
things are what I really

00:40:40.660 --> 00:40:44.020
value from big conferences,
not the content would change.

00:40:44.020 --> 00:40:46.270
So I think, yeah,
those are the kind

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:50.000
of moments that makes me feel
like, yeah, we need to do more.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:52.800
I think this has to be
not just one Indaba,

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:54.580
there should be
more in such a way

00:40:54.580 --> 00:40:59.200
that because for such a billion
population, and the huge size,

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:01.110
one conference is not just--

00:41:01.110 --> 00:41:04.390
so definitely, as Nando
was saying, is just,

00:41:04.390 --> 00:41:07.360
really, we need to
increase, and we

00:41:07.360 --> 00:41:10.210
have to bring also the
global competitors such

00:41:10.210 --> 00:41:13.030
that they can see this
talent, such that there

00:41:13.030 --> 00:41:15.580
is fair collaboration,
that people

00:41:15.580 --> 00:41:19.660
could do here something amazing
that could be highlighted.

00:41:19.660 --> 00:41:21.160
Maybe not only just
change the lives

00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:24.670
of people in Africa, but also
in the lives in the world.

00:41:24.670 --> 00:41:27.987
Because we have a lot
of different insights.

00:41:27.987 --> 00:41:30.070
And I think that's what's
also Neil Turok believes

00:41:30.070 --> 00:41:32.890
is that if we really have
the chance to really grow,

00:41:32.890 --> 00:41:36.160
it's because we see
problems differently.

00:41:36.160 --> 00:41:37.330
That's our gift.

00:41:37.330 --> 00:41:39.460
So we can't compete
with that thing--

00:41:39.460 --> 00:41:42.056
that's our real resource.

00:41:42.056 --> 00:41:43.180
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:41:43.180 --> 00:41:45.570
OK, and Jessica, what about you?

00:41:45.570 --> 00:41:48.730
JESSICA PHALAFALA: Yeah, maybe
I can just say three things.

00:41:48.730 --> 00:41:52.270
One of the first things
is the coding lab

00:41:52.270 --> 00:41:54.440
sessions have been very useful.

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:57.680
I think for those, like you
said, who are new in the field,

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:00.880
it gives you exposure
to the different topics

00:42:00.880 --> 00:42:06.400
across from CNNs, RNNs, to
just deep, feed-forward models.

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:08.300
It just gives you that
exposure you need.

00:42:08.300 --> 00:42:09.760
So if you're still
in the process

00:42:09.760 --> 00:42:14.120
of identifying your problem
and developing your problem.

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:18.350
Secondly, for those who are more
established in the industry,

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:20.860
I think it's also great
for them to just see

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:24.640
what students are getting
up to, and other researchers

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:25.420
are getting up to.

00:42:25.420 --> 00:42:29.260
I think it's been a great
time for students to also make

00:42:29.260 --> 00:42:31.570
new relationships,
and I know a couple

00:42:31.570 --> 00:42:33.717
of people who actually
found co-supervisors

00:42:33.717 --> 00:42:35.300
during the conference,
which is great.

00:42:35.300 --> 00:42:38.420
And some who have identified
potential supervisors.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:39.710
So that's just wonderful.

00:42:39.710 --> 00:42:42.130
And then within the
students, as well, we

00:42:42.130 --> 00:42:47.080
are coming up with ideas of how
to bring small-scale Indabas

00:42:47.080 --> 00:42:50.360
into the rural areas,
going back to, like I said,

00:42:50.360 --> 00:42:51.390
I was born in Polokwane.

00:42:51.390 --> 00:42:54.430
And this is something of
great interest in a part

00:42:54.430 --> 00:42:56.910
of a foundation
called [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:42:56.910 --> 00:42:58.930
And we do this at a low scale.

00:42:58.930 --> 00:43:00.430
So something like
this motivates us

00:43:00.430 --> 00:43:04.450
and gives us inspiration of how
to change the current method

00:43:04.450 --> 00:43:06.820
that we are using when we
go back into community.

00:43:06.820 --> 00:43:10.480
In one week, this conference
is able to achieve so much.

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.510
So this is a time for us to
also be inspired, as students,

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:16.060
who want to give back
to the community on what

00:43:16.060 --> 00:43:17.800
is the best way to do so?

