00:46:12.185 --> 00:46:14.480
MELANIE WARRICK: Mark, if
I'm using the cluster auto

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:17.420
scaler for Kubernetes
or GKE, how

00:46:17.420 --> 00:46:20.360
can I prevent it from removing
specific nodes from the cluster

00:46:20.360 --> 00:46:21.530
when scaling down?

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:24.269
MARK MANDEL: So, cluster
auto-scaler for Kubernetes

00:46:24.269 --> 00:46:26.060
is something I've been
digging into lately.

00:46:26.060 --> 00:46:27.559
It's actually really
cool technology

00:46:27.559 --> 00:46:30.800
in that, what it does is that
as you create more and more pods

00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:32.810
through essentially any
mechanism, deployments

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:35.390
or other such things, if
it finds that there isn't

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:38.780
enough room for those pods
to exist, it will add nodes

00:46:38.780 --> 00:46:40.610
up into a specified
limit that you add.

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:42.902
So it'll be like, oh,
you've now got 1,000,

00:46:42.902 --> 00:46:44.610
so let's make sure
there's room for that.

00:46:44.610 --> 00:46:46.310
And it'll add nodes
in a timely manner.

00:46:46.310 --> 00:46:48.685
And vise versa if you were
like, oh, I only need now 100,

00:46:48.685 --> 00:46:51.220
or I had 1,000, it will
scale it back down again.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:52.310
All very cool.

00:46:52.310 --> 00:46:54.200
But there are some
scenarios where

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:56.720
you might want to be like,
don't remove that node.

00:46:56.720 --> 00:46:58.460
Right now it's fairly
arbitrary, in terms

00:46:58.460 --> 00:46:59.810
of what it removes--
it just makes sure

00:46:59.810 --> 00:47:01.670
that there's still room
for things you need,

00:47:01.670 --> 00:47:02.720
and things like that.

00:47:02.720 --> 00:47:03.890
It will kill empty ones.

00:47:03.890 --> 00:47:06.380
But there is actually some
control that the cluster

00:47:06.380 --> 00:47:08.360
auto-scaler gives you, as well,
if there's particular nodes,

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:09.830
maybe you're running
a daemon set on,

00:47:09.830 --> 00:47:11.720
or you have specific hardware
on a particular thing

00:47:11.720 --> 00:47:13.370
you want to make sure it
doesn't get brought down.

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:14.540
And there's a couple
of interesting ways

00:47:14.540 --> 00:47:16.998
you can do this-- and I think
this is actually really cool.

00:47:16.998 --> 00:47:19.580
So at the node level, there
is an annotation you can add,

00:47:19.580 --> 00:47:21.704
basically a scaled
down disabled is true.

00:47:21.704 --> 00:47:23.370
So if you add that
to a particular node,

00:47:23.370 --> 00:47:25.160
it's just going to go, nope,
I'm not going to touch that one.

00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:25.660
It's fine.

00:47:25.660 --> 00:47:26.270
I don't care.

00:47:26.270 --> 00:47:28.640
But the other thing
worth noting is sometimes

00:47:28.640 --> 00:47:30.779
you might not know what
node you want to prevent

00:47:30.779 --> 00:47:31.820
being able to get rid of.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:34.440
What you actually may also
want to be able to do is say,

00:47:34.440 --> 00:47:37.040
hey, if there is this
particular pod running

00:47:37.040 --> 00:47:39.189
on a particular node,
don't kill that node.

00:47:39.189 --> 00:47:40.730
And so you can
actually do that too--

00:47:40.730 --> 00:47:42.030
I didn't realize this
up until recently,

00:47:42.030 --> 00:47:43.488
and I thought this
was really cool.

00:47:43.488 --> 00:47:47.030
If you add the annotation,
save to evict false on the pod,

00:47:47.030 --> 00:47:49.550
whatever node that is on,
the cluster or the scaler

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:50.510
will not remove that.

00:47:50.510 --> 00:47:51.590
Which is also cool, too.

00:47:51.590 --> 00:47:53.548
There's a bunch of other
restrictions, as well.

00:47:53.548 --> 00:47:55.864
We'll link to the FAQ
in the show notes.

00:47:55.864 --> 00:47:58.280
It'll talk things about stuff
about pod disruption budgets

00:47:58.280 --> 00:47:59.238
and a few other things.

00:47:59.238 --> 00:48:01.720
But I thought it was also just
really nice that the cluster

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:03.470
auto-scaler gave you
that kind of control,

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:05.450
both at the node level,
and at a pod level,

00:48:05.450 --> 00:48:07.370
to be able to control what
it does when it scales down.

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:09.800
Because usually, scaling down,
that's the trickier problem.

00:48:09.800 --> 00:48:10.716
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

